WEBVTT

00:10.000 --> 00:18.000
A FastAPI application starts by creating app = FastAPI(); this application instance owns the route registry and receives incoming requests.

00:18.000 --> 00:26.000
APIRouter is a smaller route registry for one module; app.include_router attaches its routes to the main FastAPI application.

00:26.000 --> 00:34.000
HTTP methods express intent, and FastAPI routing binds the combination of a method and URL path to one Python endpoint function.

00:34.000 --> 00:42.000
Python decorators wrap or register functions; @app.get("/items") and @app.post("/items") register the function immediately below as a path operation.

00:42.000 --> 00:50.000
The first decorator parameter is the URL path, while status_code, tags, summary, description, and dependencies configure route behavior and documentation grouping.

00:50.000 --> 00:58.000
A path parameter uses braces such as /items/{item_id}; FastAPI matches that location and passes the value to the same-named function argument.

00:58.000 --> 01:06.000
A function argument not present in the path is a query parameter; a default value makes it optional, and path and query arguments are parsed differently.

01:06.000 --> 01:14.000
Python type hints declare expected types. Common FastAPI route types include int, str, float, and bool on endpoint function parameters.

01:14.000 --> 01:22.000
FastAPI converts incoming text to the declared type and validates it before calling the endpoint, rejecting values that cannot be converted.

01:22.000 --> 01:30.000
Route registration happens when Python evaluates each decorator, and fixed routes should be declared before overlapping dynamic routes because matching follows declaration order.
